#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
RLG LOCK-IN ESTIMATOR BENCHMARK  --  FULL VERSION  (14 estimators, 5 families, 6 operating configurations)
=========================================================================================================
THIS FILE DOES (read top to bottom, each numbered part is a section below):
  1. CONFIG ........ every setting of the simulated gyroscope and of the experiment, in one place.
  2. SIMULATOR ..... builds fake-but-realistic gyro data with a KNOWN true rotation (so we can score).
  3. ESTIMATORS .... 14 algorithms. Each is written as a STREAMING object: it receives ONE sample at a time and keeps
                     only a small state, like it would on a real device. This is what lets us measure memory for real.
  4. REGISTRY ...... name -> (algorithm, family).  Add a new estimator here (and a grid in tune_config) to benchmark it.
  5. SCORING ....... normalised error = MAE / MAE of a lazy predictor that always guesses the mean rotation.
  6. TUNING ........ picks each estimator's settings on TRAINING seeds only (grid search + automatic grid expansion).
                     NOTE: this is tuning (choosing settings), NOT training. Nothing learns from data beforehand.
  7. EVALUATION .... scores every estimator on separate TEST seeds with the settings frozen.
  8. SWEEPS ........ stress tests: corrupted readings (glitches) and weaker dither.
  9. COST .......... speed (microseconds per step), lag, and ENERGY (measured with RAPL if the machine has it,
                     otherwise estimated from CPU time x watts and clearly labelled as an estimate).
 10. MEMORY ........ real bytes of working state each estimator holds, plus transient workspace (tracemalloc).
 11. LOW-MEMORY .... (a) what happens if numbers are stored in 32-bit instead of 64-bit,
                     (b) what happens under a fixed memory budget (window-based methods must shrink; big ones may not fit).
 12. REPORT ........ markdown tables, generated findings, figures.
 13. SELFTEST ...... checks that the code is correct (matches the earlier v16 numbers, streaming == batch formulas).

USAGE
  pip install numpy scipy matplotlib
  python3 rlg_bench_full.py describe
  python3 rlg_bench_full.py selftest
  python3 rlg_bench_full.py all            # full paper-style run, about 1 hour on one CPU core
  python3 rlg_bench_full.py all --quick    # warm-start tuning (small grids around earlier optimum), about 25 minutes
  stages: tune | eval | sweeps | costs | memory | lowmem | report   [mild|harsh]   [--quick] [--resume]
ENV:  RLG_MODEL=base (simpler gyro)  RLG_SMOKE=1 (tiny run to test the pipeline)  RLG_OUT=<dir>  RLG_WATTS=<per-core watts>
"""
